---
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TweetONE CoreXY"
  description="A high-speed CoreXY conversion built from two Ender 3 printers, tuned for Triple-Z motion, CANbus, and practical workshop engineering."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Project case study"
    title="TweetONE CoreXY"
    subtitle="A dual-Ender 3 / Ender 3 V2 conversion into a high-speed CoreXY printer with Triple-Z motion, linear rails, and a CANbus-based control stack."
    compact
  >
    <div class="content has-text-left mt-5">
      <p><strong>Son</strong> — Lead Engineer &amp; Mechatronics Architect</p>
      <p><strong>Father</strong> — Project Lead &amp; Technical Mentor</p>
    </div>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section aw-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="columns is-vcentered">
        <div class="column is-7">
          <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Overview</p>
          <h2 class="title is-2">The idea behind the build</h2>
          <div class="content">
            <p>
              TweetONE began as a family engineering project built around a simple challenge:
              take the most useful parts of two older Creality Ender 3 machines and turn them into a
              fast, stiff, and precise CoreXY platform designed for real workshop use.
            </p>
            <p>
              The concept drew inspiration from the Duender CoreXY, Ender NG, Mercury One.1, and
              RatRig systems, but pushed further with linear rails, triple-Z motion, a CANbus toolboard,
              and a modern Klipper toolchain. The target was not just a clean printer, but a machine that
              balances print quality, rigidity, and usable speed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-5">
          <div class="box aw-hero-box">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Phase 0</p>
            <p class="mb-2"><strong>Goal:</strong> prove that one Raspberry Pi 4B can run dual-MCU Klipper reliably.</p>
            <p class="mb-2"><strong>Experiment:</strong> connect both Creality v4.2.2 boards at once over USB.</p>
            <p><strong>Result:</strong> stable XY + independent Triple-Z control before the mechanical rebuild began.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section aw-section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">RPi bring-up</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">Multi-MCU Klipper from a single Raspberry Pi</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">            
             <p><strong>Step 1:</strong> flash MainsailOS or a BTT CB1 image and configure Wi-Fi / hostname.</p>
             <p><strong>Step 2:</strong> bring the Pi up over SSH and locate the board serial IDs.</p>
             <p><strong>Step 3:</strong> compile Klipper with <code>make menuconfig</code> for an STM32F103 and a GD32F303 board.</p>
             <p><strong>Step 4:</strong> remove 5V from the USB data cable to eliminate backfeeding and serial instability.</p>
             <p><strong>Step 5:</strong> wire the right MCU serial addresses into <code>printer.cfg</code> and verify motion before final assembly.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">system.cfg</p>
            <pre><code>WIFI_SSID="YourHomeWiFi"
WIFI_PASSWD="VerySecretPassword"
hostname="TweetONE-M"</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">make menuconfig</p>
            <pre><code># STM32F103 board
Micro-controller Architecture: STMicroelectronics STM32
Processor model: STM32F103
Bootloader offset: 28KiB bootloader
Communication interface: Serial (USART1 PA10/PA9)

# GD32F303 board
Disable SWD at startup: enabled</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">USB 5V tape-cut fix</p>
            <pre><code>USB-A connector layout:
[ 1: 5V (tape / remove) ] [ 2: Data- ] [ 3: Data+ ] [ 4: GND ]

# Only the data pairs and GND remain connected.
# This removes 5V backfeeding and stabilizes the serial bus.</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Device discovery</p>
            <pre><code>ls /dev/serial/by-id/*

/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_stm32f103xe_36FFD5054252303728641943-if00
/dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_gd32f303_51FFD8054252383832541143-if00</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">printer.cfg</p>
            <pre><code>[mcu]
serial: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_stm32f103xe_36FFD5054252303728641943-if00

[mcu GD]
serial: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_gd32f303_51FFD8054252383832541143-if00</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Hardware &amp; BOM</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">Profile sizes, rails, and motion hardware</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="box aw-card mb-5">
        <table class="table is-fullwidth is-striped is-narrow">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Component</th>
              <th>Qty</th>
              <th>Origin</th>
              <th>Use in TweetONE</th>
              <th>Note</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>4040 profile (290 mm)</td>
              <td>4</td>
              <td>Ender 3 + Ender 3 V2</td>
              <td>Lower rectangular frame</td>
              <td>Very stiff base geometry</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2040 profile (400 mm)</td>
              <td>4</td>
              <td>Ender 3 + Ender 3 V2</td>
              <td>Main vertical frame members</td>
              <td>Maintains full print height</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2040 profile (350 mm)</td>
              <td>2</td>
              <td>Added / modified Y-axis</td>
              <td>Linear rail guides</td>
              <td>Validated against Y-axis geometry</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2020 profile (300 mm)</td>
              <td>1</td>
              <td>Top bridge</td>
              <td>X gantry cross member</td>
              <td>Trimmed for exact 300 mm length</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2020 profile (350 mm)</td>
              <td>3</td>
              <td>Added / reused</td>
              <td>Triple-Z rail support</td>
              <td>Used for the Z-axis layout</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Additional hardware</p>
            <ul class="aw-check-list">
              <li>THK SRS 12GN linear rail (310 mm) for the X axis</li>
              <li>MGN9H / MGN12H rails for the Y axis</li>
              <li>F623RS bearings and 2GT belts</li>
              <li>Brass heat-set inserts for structural joints</li>
              <li>POM anti-backlash nuts for the Z screws</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Practical note</p>
            <p>
              The most important design decision was moving away from V-wheels and toward linear motion,
              better-defined alignment, and stiffer gantry geometry. That allowed the printer to keep a good
              balance between speed and repeatability.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section aw-section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Development log</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">The build timeline from 2025 to 2026</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Phase 1</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Onshape design and rail selection</h3>
            <p><strong>December 2025.</strong> We measured the available profiles from both Ender 3 printers and began designing the first CoreXY geometry around THK SRS rails and a tighter gantry footprint.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Phase 2</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Dual-MCU Klipper bring-up</h3>
            <p><strong>January 2026.</strong> One Raspberry Pi 4B successfully ran two Creality v4.2.2 boards in parallel, proving the multi-MCU concept before machining and assembly began.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Phase 3</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Origami vs. industrial skeleton</h3>
            <p><strong>February to June 2026.</strong> We explored the Origami concept and an industrial skeleton layout, then developed custom belt tensioners and refined the mechanical system.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Phase 4</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Z-Tilt and first successful print</h3>
            <p><strong>July to August 2026.</strong> The Z-axis was calibrated with a measured 0.005 mm plane offset, and the printer completed its first successful first layer and print.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Phase 5</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">BTT Manta M8P v2.0 + EBB36 CANbus + DragonBurner</h3>
            <p><strong>August to September 2026.</strong> We upgraded the motion controller to BTT Manta M8P v2.0 with CB1, then moved to a CANbus toolboard using the BTT EBB36 and DragonBurner. Input shaper measurements then confirmed the final tuning envelope: the Y axis at 53.8 Hz and the X axis at 69.8 Hz, with safe acceleration limits around 8500 mm/s² and 14400 mm/s².</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Troubleshooting</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">The dead ends we solved</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Problem 1</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Inverted enable pins and swapped step/direction lines</h3>
            <p><strong>Cause:</strong> the Creality v4.2.2 GPIO mappings were not aligned with the expected Klipper pin logic.</p>
            <p><strong>Fix:</strong> invert the enable pin with <code>!</code> and correct the pin mapping in the printer config.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Problem 2</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">USB 5V backfeeding</h3>
            <p><strong>Cause:</strong> the Raspberry Pi was feeding 5V back into the Creality boards via VBUS.</p>
            <p><strong>Fix:</strong> tape over the 5V pin on both USB cables so the bus carries only Data+ / Data- / GND.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Problem 3</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">Y-axis carriage collision</h3>
            <p><strong>Cause:</strong> the printed holder geometry left too little clearance for the MGN carriage.</p>
            <p><strong>Fix:</strong> shorten the 3D model and reprint the structure to regain safe carriage travel.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Problem 4</p>
            <h3 class="title is-5">BTT Eddy Duo temperature drift</h3>
            <p><strong>Cause:</strong> the inductive probe changed behavior with bed and probe temperature shifts.</p>
            <p><strong>Fix:</strong> complete a full <code>TEMPERATURE_PROBE_CALIBRATE</code> routine and build a temperature compensation curve.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section aw-section-alt">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Research &amp; experiments</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">The technical experiments behind the final machine</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <h3 class="title is-5">Anti-backlash POM vs. brass nuts</h3>
            <p>
              Standard brass trapezoidal nuts have backlash. When the lead screw flexes, that mechanical play shows up as visible Z-wobble on tall prints. We compared that with POM anti-backlash nuts, which apply preload and reduce free motion without needing frequent lubrication.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="column is-6">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <h3 class="title is-5">Digital GT2 belt tension meter</h3>
            <p>
              Uneven belt tension in a CoreXY machine can skew the geometry and create circularity errors. We built a simple tension meter using a strain gauge from a kitchen scale, 3D-printed parts, and a spring system to keep both belts balanced and repeatable.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section aw-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="aw-section-head">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-2">Reference configs</p>
        <h2 class="title is-2">Practical examples and external references</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="columns is-multiline">
        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">system.cfg</p>
            <pre><code># /boot/system.cfg
WIFI_SSID="MyHomeWiFi"
WIFI_PASSWD="MySecretPassword"
hostname="TweetONE-M"</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Multi-MCU printer.cfg</p>
            <pre><code>[mcu]
serial: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_stm32f103xe_12345678-if00

[mcu GD]
serial: /dev/serial/by-id/usb-Klipper_gd32f303_87654321-if00

[printer]
kinematics: corexy
max_velocity: 350
max_accel: 10000
max_z_velocity: 15
max_z_accel: 200</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="column is-12">
          <div class="box aw-card">
            <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Sensorless homing for BTT Manta M8P v2.0</p>
            <pre><code>[stepper_x]
endstop_pin: tmc2209_stepper_x:virtual_endstop
homing_retract_dist: 0

[tmc2209 stepper_x]
diag_pin: ^PF4
run_current: 0.800
driver_SGTHRS: 100</code></pre>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="box aw-card mt-5">
        <p class="aw-eyebrow mb-3">Reference links</p>
        <ul class="aw-check-list">
          <li><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@Irbis3D" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Irbis3D Duender CoreXY</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://cad.onshape.com/documents/966ba2a55f3eaeb8f0d4e8fd/w/0c1629acfaec8e996b2f85eb/e/0f8e7f946d3fac114d64b84e" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Onshape CAD — Duender CoreXY</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.printables.com/model/1282344-duender-mgn12h-edition" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Printables — Duender MGN12H Edition</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/chirpy2605/voron/blob/main/V0/Dragon_Burner/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DragonBurner</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://global.bttwiki.com/M8P-V2_0.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">BTT Manta M8P v2.0 wiki</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://os.ratrig.com/docs/toolboards/btt/ebb-36-12/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">BTT EBB36 CANbus docs</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://docs.vorondesign.com/tuning/sensorless.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Voron sensorless homing tuning</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    eyebrow="Keep learning"
    title="The project is still evolving."
    text="Browse the build notes in the blog or get in touch to discuss your own 3D prototype, printer upgrade, or product challenge."
    ctaLabel="Read the blog"
    ctaHref="/blog"
  />
</BaseLayout>
